WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.780
- I am 35,000 feet above
the North Atlantic,

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:05.580
somewhere just south of
the tip of Greenland.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.740
And there's no radar coverage up here,

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.640
there's no real-time view
for an air traffic controller to know

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.480
where all the hundreds of planes
sharing this airspace currently are.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.680
So, how do we keep safe?

00:00:16.687 --> 00:00:19.591
How do we avoid getting
a bit too close to any of them?

00:00:19.591 --> 00:00:21.851
The answer is down at my destination.

00:00:21.851 --> 00:00:24.749
GPS: "Continue for 205 miles."

00:00:24.749 --> 00:00:28.676
This is the Nav Canada Area Control Centre
in Gander, Newfoundland.

00:00:28.676 --> 00:00:30.066
And if you're over the North Atlantic,

00:00:30.066 --> 00:00:32.051
these are some of the
folks keeping you safe.

00:00:32.051 --> 00:00:33.688
- The North Atlantic
tracks are the airways

00:00:33.688 --> 00:00:37.719
that we develop each day
for our eastbound aircraft

00:00:37.719 --> 00:00:41.047
who fly across from us
over to the UK area.

00:00:41.047 --> 00:00:43.259
The westbound tracks are produced

00:00:43.259 --> 00:00:46.386
by our counterpart over in Scotland
via Prestwick Centre.

00:00:46.386 --> 00:00:49.224
The way the North Atlantic works,
there's basically two main airflows.

00:00:49.224 --> 00:00:51.491
During the night time,
our night time here,

00:00:51.491 --> 00:00:55.827
most of the aircraft leave North America
and fly across to the UK.

00:00:55.827 --> 00:00:58.319
And during the daytime,
most of those same aircraft return.

00:00:58.319 --> 00:01:00.156
It's a 24 hour operation obviously.

00:01:00.156 --> 00:01:03.313
We start with our day-shift,
comes in early in the morning.

00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:06.256
We'll ask all these aircraft to send in
their preferred routes for that night.

00:01:06.256 --> 00:01:07.700
So I'll take that into consideration.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.297
I'll also look at weather models.

00:01:09.297 --> 00:01:13.154
Eastbound aircraft, all the aircraft want
to get into the main jet stream

00:01:13.154 --> 00:01:16.098
to pick up that tailwind
so they can save money and fuel and time.

00:01:16.098 --> 00:01:19.558
From all that, we'll develop
a set of tracks based on that flow,

00:01:19.558 --> 00:01:24.045
and once the tracks are published
in the morning, airlines then have

00:01:24.045 --> 00:01:26.007
anywhere from 8 to 10, 12 hours

00:01:26.007 --> 00:01:28.269
to decide on what track they
want to follow that night.

00:01:28.269 --> 00:01:31.654
- The tracks can look curved and inefficient
on the flat map projections we're used to

00:01:31.654 --> 00:01:34.026
but you've got to remember,
they're great circle routes.

00:01:34.026 --> 00:01:37.411
They're the shortest, and cheapest way
around the globe of the earth.

00:01:37.411 --> 00:01:39.526
And also, the tracks
are three dimensional.

00:01:39.526 --> 00:01:42.175
Planes can be separated because
they're on a different track,

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.600
or because they're on different positions
on the same track,

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.469
or by altitude.

00:01:46.469 --> 00:01:48.755
- It's a requirement for an aircraft
to give us a position report

00:01:48.755 --> 00:01:50.117
every 10 degrees of longitude.

00:01:50.117 --> 00:01:52.972
And we take that information,
and we apply it to the aircraft's route,

00:01:52.972 --> 00:01:54.180
and we update our system,

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.730
and we can kinda predict
where he's gonna be too.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:58.904
When there's no radar coverage,
there's two main forms of communicating

00:01:58.904 --> 00:02:00.536
with the aircraft over the Atlantic Ocean.

00:02:00.536 --> 00:02:03.054
We can go through our radio operators,
our flight service operators,

00:02:03.054 --> 00:02:04.407
and contact them through HF.

00:02:04.407 --> 00:02:06.521
The easiest way for us, I guess,

00:02:06.521 --> 00:02:08.412
is like a form of text message,
you could call it.

00:02:08.412 --> 00:02:09.539
I can just click on a button,

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:12.122
and I can tell exactly where that aircraft is
at that point in time.

00:02:12.122 --> 00:02:13.773
It's very precise, even without radar.

00:02:13.773 --> 00:02:15.746
As a normal standard for our tracks,

00:02:15.746 --> 00:02:18.249
side-by-side, which is
our lateral separation,

00:02:18.249 --> 00:02:19.909
we're running 60 nautical miles apart.

00:02:19.909 --> 00:02:22.206
As time progresses,
airlines get better equipped,

00:02:22.206 --> 00:02:25.130
we'll probably have all our tracks
at 30 miles apart from each other.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:27.789
Longitudinal, which is one
aircraft behind the other,

00:02:27.789 --> 00:02:29.461
we're running 10 minutes.

00:02:29.461 --> 00:02:32.774
We can reduce that down to a
five minute standard if airliners are equipped.

00:02:32.774 --> 00:02:35.042
And vertical, we're using 1,000 feet.

00:02:35.042 --> 00:02:36.966
Some nights, you can just
sit out on your patio

00:02:36.966 --> 00:02:38.763
on a nice clear night, and
you can see numerous aircraft,

00:02:38.763 --> 00:02:40.679
and they look like they're all
on top of each other, but basically,

00:02:40.679 --> 00:02:43.532
they're all at least 1,000 feet apart,
if not more.

00:02:43.532 --> 00:02:45.919
They just have to be separated
in one dimension basically.

00:02:45.919 --> 00:02:49.066
As soon as an aircraft gets too tight
to another aircraft, we interject and say,

00:02:49.066 --> 00:02:50.897
okay, we need to slow you down,
or speed you up,

00:02:50.897 --> 00:02:52.503
traffic permitting obviously, right?

00:02:52.503 --> 00:02:55.099
If a plane has an emergency,
they have contingency procedures.

00:02:55.099 --> 00:02:56.356
They have their TCAS.

00:02:56.356 --> 00:02:58.573
TCAS is their
Traffic Collision Avoidance System.

00:02:58.573 --> 00:03:00.204
For instance, if you had two aircraft

00:03:00.204 --> 00:03:01.914
and an aircraft did something
it wasn't supposed to do,

00:03:01.914 --> 00:03:03.309
climbed up into another aircraft,

00:03:03.309 --> 00:03:06.186
that plane has a TCAS system that says,
okay you have incoming traffic,

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:09.984
you have to climb, or descend,
or whatever the resolution is, right?

00:03:09.984 --> 00:03:12.522
So as soon as we're made aware
of an emergency situation,

00:03:12.522 --> 00:03:15.118
we look at separation, we try
to move what aircraft we can,

00:03:15.118 --> 00:03:18.625
and we provide a conflict free
clearance for that aircraft,

00:03:18.625 --> 00:03:20.320
safety being the number one thing here.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.160
- Thank you very much to
everyone at Nav Canada,

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:24.150
and at the Gander Area Control Centre.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.440
You can find out more about them
and the tracks at the links in the description.

